    "bounding box" : [ 70.866, 724.301, 288.9, 743.554 ],
    "heading level" : 2,
    "font" : "LinLibertineTB",
    "font size" : 14.346,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "7.1 Asteroidal triple-free graphs"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 9,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 70.866, 631.567, 524.414, 720.318 ],
    "font" : "LinLibertineT",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "An asteroidal triple in a graph G is a set T of 3 vertices such that G[T] is independent and every pair of vertices in T can be joined by a path that avoids the neighborhood of the third vertex in T. A graph G is asteroidal triple-free (AT-free) if G does not contain an asteroidal triple. Various classes of graphs are known to be AT-free, for example, interval graphs, cocomporability graphs and permutation graphs (see e.g. [COS97]). We show that all these classes have a bounded ratio by proving the following theorem."
  }, {
    "type" : "list",
    "pdfua_tag" : "L",
    "id" : 10,
    "level" : "1",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 70.866, 351.229, 524.406, 622.354 ],
    "numbering style" : "arabic numbers",
    "number of list items" : 3,
    "list items" : [ {
      "type" : "list item",
      "pdfua_tag" : "LI",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 70.866, 582.092, 461.086, 622.354 ],
      "font" : "LinLibertineTB",
      "font size" : 11.955,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "Theorem 7.1. For every asteroidal triple-free graph G, we have γ(G) ⩽ 3 · ρ(G). Proof. Our proof is based on the following property.",
      "kids" : [ ]
    }, {
      "type" : "list item",
      "pdfua_tag" : "LI",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 70.866, 547.223, 524.406, 577.522 ],
      "font" : "LinLibertineTB",
      "font size" : 11.955,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "Theorem 7.2. [COS97] Every connected asteroidal triple-free graph contains a pair of vertices such that any path between this pair of vertices is dominating.",
      "kids" : [ {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 1,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 70.866, 461.269, 524.409, 543.98 ],
        "font" : "LinLibertineT",
        "font size" : 11.955,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "Let G be an AT-free graph. By Theorem 7.2 there is a pair of vertices u,v ∈ V (G) such that any path between u and v is dominating. Let Π = π1,π2,...,πp be the shortest path between u and v. Thus, we have γ(G) ⩽ p. On the other hand as Π is a shortest path in G, by taking the set of vertices {π3i+1 | 0 ⩽ i ⩽ ⌊p/3⌋ − 1} we get a packing in G. Therefore, ρ(G) ⩾ ⌊p/3⌋ ⩾ ⌊γ(G)/3⌋."
      }, {
        "type" : "table",
        "pdfua_tag" : "Table",
        "id" : 2,
        "level" : "2",
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 515.775, 472.746, 523.745, 480.816 ],
        "number of rows" : 1,
        "number of columns" : 1,
        "rows" : [ {
          "type" : "table row",
          "row number" : 1,
          "cells" : [ {
            "type" : "table cell",
